WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:02.010
- [Narrator] These were the CEOs

00:00:02.010 --> 00:00:05.910
of today's five biggest
banks in the US in 2006.

00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:08.010
This is that group in 2011,

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:11.670
in 2016, and in 2021.

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:14.310
You'll notice one face remained the same.

00:00:14.310 --> 00:00:18.180
This man, JPMorgan Chase CEO, Jamie Dimon

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:20.790
- He is the bank, the
bank is him in some ways

00:00:20.790 --> 00:00:22.980
- [Narrator] Over his
tenure, Dimon has become

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:25.050
the go-to voice for banks.

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:26.370
- We need healthy, big banks.

00:00:26.370 --> 00:00:28.050
We have the best banking
system in the world.

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:31.110
- [Narrator] Here's how Dimon's
strategy for managing risk

00:00:31.110 --> 00:00:34.560
has made him the most powerful
player in the industry

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:36.570
and how he's used that position

00:00:36.570 --> 00:00:39.630
to steer the entire banking world.

00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:41.160
Dimon built his reputation

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:43.920
over a career that stretches back decades.

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:46.800
- He has shaped how the
big banks think about

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:48.510
what is safe and what isn't safe.

00:00:48.510 --> 00:00:50.820
You can look around and
see that his lieutenants

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:53.880
and former executives run a
whole bunch of other banks,

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:55.380
and they all were schooled

00:00:55.380 --> 00:00:57.600
at how to run a bank by Jamie Dimon.

00:00:57.600 --> 00:00:58.830
- He's not a reckless banker.

00:00:58.830 --> 00:01:01.230
I think he understands you need
to take risks to make money

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:02.880
but you also need to understand the risk

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:04.440
and manage the risk.

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:07.020
- [Narrator] The center of
Dimon's risk management strategy

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:10.020
is a concept he calls the
fortress balance sheet.

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:11.490
- I always thought you should
have a fortress balance sheet

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:13.560
in this business and maybe some of yours

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:15.000
because bad things happen.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:16.350
- [Narrator] The idea is to build up

00:01:16.350 --> 00:01:18.540
a cushion of assets and capital

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:20.340
to protect consumer's deposits

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:23.130
if the bank gets hit by
unexpected shockwaves

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:24.390
- And most banks will build the moat

00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:27.240
and build up their walls
and say, we're protected.

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:28.620
But what Jamie Dimon says is,

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:31.770
okay, but those walls might
be made of like paper mache,

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:34.020
so on top of that wall that
all the other banks have,

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:35.640
we're gonna add a higher wall

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:37.470
and then we're gonna
add some terace up here,

00:01:37.470 --> 00:01:39.120
and then we're gonna add
some people walking around

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:41.130
all the time making sure
that everything is safe

00:01:41.130 --> 00:01:42.150
all the way down.

00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:44.850
- Is it fair to say that
JPMorgan could have losses

00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:47.340
of a half a trillion dollars
or a trillion dollars?

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:50.763
- Not unless this earth is hit by a moon.

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:53.513
- Okay.

00:01:53.513 --> 00:01:55.590
- He doesn't care about
this quarter's results.

00:01:55.590 --> 00:01:57.300
He doesn't care about next year's results.

00:01:57.300 --> 00:02:00.180
He cares about making
sure the bank is safe.

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:02.250
Partly he gets to say
that 'cause he still makes

00:02:02.250 --> 00:02:04.590
a ton of money on everything he does,

00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:06.780
but it's worked.

00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:10.110
- [Narrator] That strategy
helped JPMorgan Chase and Dimon

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:14.370
emerge from the 2008 financial
crisis relatively unscathed.

00:02:14.370 --> 00:02:17.370
And even in a position
to assist the government.

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:18.690
- We were called upon to take actions

00:02:18.690 --> 00:02:20.130
to help stabilize the system.

00:02:20.130 --> 00:02:22.800
- [Narrator] In the aftermath,
the bank put up big bids

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:25.320
to acquire two failing
financial institutions

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:29.250
from the government, Bear
Stearns and Washington Mutual.

00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:31.080
- So he makes these smart acquisitions

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:32.430
that were beneficial to him

00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:34.530
and beneficial to the government.

00:02:34.530 --> 00:02:35.430
- [Narrator] At the time,

00:02:35.430 --> 00:02:37.443
Sheila Bair was the head of the FDIC.

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:40.710
She orchestrated bids
for Washington Mutual,

00:02:40.710 --> 00:02:43.290
the largest bank to fail in US history.

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:45.540
- We were having a hard
time getting bidders in,

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:47.700
a lot of banks just didn't wanna touch it.

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:49.260
So he did not withstand the fact

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:50.580
we were running a competitive process,

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:52.410
he still came in a bid, thank goodness.

00:02:52.410 --> 00:02:55.230
And as I suspected he would,

00:02:55.230 --> 00:02:57.600
he did win the bid by
a pretty large margin.

00:02:57.600 --> 00:02:58.920
- [Narrator] Even with debts incurred

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:01.410
and lawsuits following
from those purchases,

00:03:01.410 --> 00:03:02.850
the moves helped Chase grow

00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:06.150
to be the largest bank
by deposits by 2011.

00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:08.160
- He's gonna look at something
like Washington Mutual

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:11.190
and say, that's gonna
give us more deposits

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:12.510
and more customers.

00:03:12.510 --> 00:03:14.250
And we're getting it
at a super cheap price,

00:03:14.250 --> 00:03:16.230
the government's supposed
to share the losses.

00:03:16.230 --> 00:03:18.287
This is a risk that is worth taking.

00:03:18.287 --> 00:03:20.880
- Yowamu put us into California

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:22.200
where we really were in Florida

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:23.880
we had very small presence.

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:25.050
Washington State

00:03:25.050 --> 00:03:27.630
- JPMorgan grew even larger in May

00:03:27.630 --> 00:03:29.760
after it bought First Republic Bank,

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:32.970
the second largest bank
to fail in US history.

00:03:32.970 --> 00:03:34.920
But this also raised concern

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:37.200
that the bank was too big to fail.

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:39.210
Meaning that if it were to collapse

00:03:39.210 --> 00:03:42.450
it would have a devastating
impact on the economy.

00:03:42.450 --> 00:03:43.750
- Are you too big to fail?

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:47.400
- I don't know what
that word means anymore.

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:48.540
- JPMorgan Chase does benefit

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:51.030
from the perception of
being too big to fail.

00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:52.230
I know they don't like to hear that.

00:03:52.230 --> 00:03:53.670
They think they're very well run

00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:55.230
and they are a great bank, but I'm sorry,

00:03:55.230 --> 00:03:56.370
they get a benefit.

00:03:56.370 --> 00:03:58.620
Nobody thinks even if JPMorgan
Chase got into trouble,

00:03:58.620 --> 00:04:01.200
nobody thinks it would ever be closed.

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:03.750
- [Narrator] But Dimon has
made missteps in the past.

00:04:03.750 --> 00:04:07.590
In 2012, the bank lost
more than $6 billion

00:04:07.590 --> 00:04:10.980
after an employee made
a series of risky bets.

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:14.481
- This transaction that you said morphed

00:04:14.481 --> 00:04:15.720
what did it morph into?

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:17.190
Russian roulette.

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:19.230
- It morphed into
something I can't justify

00:04:19.230 --> 00:04:20.910
that was just too risky for our company.

00:04:20.910 --> 00:04:22.710
- He does impose accountability.

00:04:22.710 --> 00:04:24.120
In the context of risk,

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:26.430
that could be one of your
best risk management tools

00:04:26.430 --> 00:04:28.110
is to make sure, you
know, tell your people,

00:04:28.110 --> 00:04:29.580
go out and take some
risk, make some money,

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:31.140
understand the risk, manage the risk,

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:33.780
stay within our risk
appetite and understand,

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:35.700
you know, if you take stupid risk,

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:37.233
you're gonna be accountable.

00:04:37.233 --> 00:04:41.220
- I said, don't put me on
the pedestal, just stop that.

00:04:41.220 --> 00:04:44.700
Companies make mistakes
and CEOs make mistakes.

00:04:44.700 --> 00:04:46.440
- [Narrator] While
Dimon's management style

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:49.950
has helped the bank grow even
larger than its competitors,

00:04:49.950 --> 00:04:53.070
it's also expanded his
role in the industry.

00:04:53.070 --> 00:04:54.480
- If you're the treasury secretary

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:56.340
and there's something broken
in the banking industry,

00:04:56.340 --> 00:04:57.900
Jamie Dimon is your first call.

00:04:57.900 --> 00:04:59.760
- [Narrator] Treasury
secretary, Janet Yellen

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:02.160
called Dimon to kickstart First republic's

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:03.750
initial rescue effort.

00:05:03.750 --> 00:05:04.770
- It bought the bank time.

00:05:04.770 --> 00:05:06.750
It did seem to help the crisis

00:05:06.750 --> 00:05:10.110
and really other CEOs and
other banks have been like

00:05:10.110 --> 00:05:10.943
it was Jamie.

00:05:10.943 --> 00:05:12.060
- [Narrator] And over the years,

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:15.810
Dimon has become the go-to
voice for banks in Washington.

00:05:15.810 --> 00:05:17.220
- Members of the committee,

00:05:17.220 --> 00:05:20.190
I appreciate the opportunity
to talk about JPMorgan Chase,

00:05:20.190 --> 00:05:21.720
the role of America's largest banks

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:24.450
as a force for good for
the country, its citizens

00:05:24.450 --> 00:05:25.800
and the global economy.

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:27.060
- When they all go to testify

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:28.860
pretty much every year
in front of Congress,

00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:29.910
the other CEOs are like,

00:05:29.910 --> 00:05:31.529
let's let Jamie answer the questions,

00:05:31.529 --> 00:05:33.570
we'll let him be the guy out front.

00:05:33.570 --> 00:05:36.180
And part of that's because
like he's built this reputation

00:05:36.180 --> 00:05:38.730
and he's less worried about losing his job

00:05:38.730 --> 00:05:39.930
or getting regulators mad at him.

00:05:39.930 --> 00:05:42.090
- I think it will put
smaller banks in a position

00:05:42.090 --> 00:05:43.830
that when a crisis hits,

00:05:43.830 --> 00:05:45.960
they will virtually have to stop lending

00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:47.700
because putting up those
reserves will be too much

00:05:47.700 --> 00:05:49.350
at precisely the wrong time.

00:05:49.350 --> 00:05:50.580
So I do think this becomes an issue

00:05:50.580 --> 00:05:53.190
for you all to reconsider.

00:05:53.190 --> 00:05:55.620
- [Narrator] And Dimon
is known to be outspoken

00:05:55.620 --> 00:05:57.213
in public statements too.

00:05:58.050 --> 00:06:00.600
- Bitcoin itself is a hyped up fraud.

00:06:00.600 --> 00:06:01.980
- [Dimon] It's almost an embarrassment

00:06:01.980 --> 00:06:04.260
being an American citizen
traveling around the world

00:06:04.260 --> 00:06:05.730
and listening to the stupid shit

00:06:05.730 --> 00:06:06.960
we have to deal with in this country.

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:08.250
- You ask me, I'll tell you my opinion,

00:06:08.250 --> 00:06:11.010
that's not a JPMorgan opinion
stuff like that but...

00:06:11.010 --> 00:06:13.650
- He is the same person
privately and publicly.

00:06:13.650 --> 00:06:16.290
Like he'll curse, he'll
get excited about things,

00:06:16.290 --> 00:06:20.610
he'll dismiss other
things, like he is Jamie

00:06:20.610 --> 00:06:22.590
- [Narrator] A spokesperson
for JPMorgan Chase

00:06:22.590 --> 00:06:25.773
and Jamie Dimon declined
to comment for this video.

00:06:26.790 --> 00:06:30.180
After nearly two decades
atop JPMorgan Chase,

00:06:30.180 --> 00:06:32.610
there have been questions
about who could fill his shoes

00:06:32.610 --> 00:06:34.530
when he decides to move on.

00:06:34.530 --> 00:06:37.500
- He truly has no hobbies
from what I can tell.

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:39.240
Even his friends are like
he doesn't do anything

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:40.530
other than run the bank.

00:06:40.530 --> 00:06:43.530
- [Narrator] But Dimon doesn't
plan on going anywhere soon.

00:06:43.530 --> 00:06:45.540
- [Dimon] I'm not gonna play golf.

00:06:45.540 --> 00:06:49.110
I love my country, I love my
company, I love my family.

00:06:49.110 --> 00:06:51.480
I'm gonna do something,
I can't do this forever,

00:06:51.480 --> 00:06:52.473
I know that.

00:06:53.700 --> 00:06:55.350
But my intensity is the same.

00:06:55.350 --> 00:06:57.870
- A normal person might say
it's time to hang up the shoes

00:06:57.870 --> 00:07:00.423
but Jamie Dimon is not
necessarily a normal person.

